CAT = "mlsMicro"; SEG = "MIC"
Q = [
('For adult blood cultures, which factor matters most for recovering the organism?',
 'Volume of blood collected', ['Time of day of the draw', 'Brand of skin antiseptic', 'Incubation beyond 7 days'],
 'The number of organisms in bacteremia is often low, so yield rises with volume; about 20 mL per set is usual for adults. Most pathogens grow within 5 days.'),
('Streptococci from endocarditis grow only as satellites around Staphylococcus aureus. They need:',
 'Pyridoxal (vitamin B6)', ['Hemin (X factor)', 'NAD (V factor)', 'Sheep blood and iron'],
 'Abiotrophia and Granulicatella, the nutritionally variant streptococci, need pyridoxal, which S. aureus releases. X and V factors are the Haemophilus requirements.'),
('Culture-negative endocarditis is classically linked with which slow-growing group?',
 'HACEK organisms', ['Viridans streptococci', 'Enterobacterales', 'Bacteroides fragilis group'],
 'Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella and Kingella are fastidious gram-negative rods. Viridans streptococci cause endocarditis but grow readily in routine bottles.'),
('A tiny gram-negative coccobacillus from blood grows slowly and is urease positive within minutes. The patient drinks raw goat milk. Suspect:',
 'Brucella', ['Haemophilus influenzae', 'Pasteurella multocida', 'Bordetella pertussis'],
 'Brucella is a BSL-3 agent spread by unpasteurized dairy products, and its rapid urease is a key clue. Suspected isolates go to the Laboratory Response Network rather than to further workup.'),
('An asaccharolytic, DNase-positive gram-negative diplococcus from sputum is most likely:',
 'Moraxella catarrhalis', ['Neisseria gonorrhoeae', 'Neisseria lactamica', 'Kingella kingae'],
 'M. catarrhalis uses no sugars and is DNase and butyrate esterase positive. Neisseria species produce acid from at least glucose.'),
('A gram-negative diplococcus produces acid from glucose and maltose but not lactose or sucrose. It is:',
 'Neisseria meningitidis', ['Neisseria gonorrhoeae', 'Neisseria lactamica', 'Moraxella catarrhalis'],
 'N. meningitidis uses glucose and maltose; N. gonorrhoeae uses glucose only; N. lactamica adds lactose; Moraxella uses none of them.'),
('Cultures from an infected CSF shunt are held longer than usual to recover:',
 'Cutibacterium acnes', ['Listeria monocytogenes', 'Streptococcus agalactiae', 'Escherichia coli'],
 'Cutibacterium is a slow-growing anaerobic skin organism that colonizes shunt hardware. The meningitis pathogens listed grow within a day or two.'),
('Black colonies on Bacteroides bile esculin agar from an abdominal abscess suggest:',
 'Bacteroides fragilis group', ['Clostridium perfringens', 'Prevotella melaninogenica', 'Fusobacterium nucleatum'],
 'The B. fragilis group grows in 20% bile and hydrolyzes esculin, blackening the medium. Pigmented Prevotella turns black on blood agar but is inhibited by bile.'),
('A gram-positive rod with round terminal spores (drumsticks) from a wound is most likely:',
 'Clostridium tetani', ['Clostridium perfringens', 'Clostridium septicum', 'Bacillus anthracis'],
 'C. tetani forms round terminal spores that give a drumstick look. C. perfringens rarely shows spores, and B. anthracis is aerobic.'),
('Legionella urinary antigen tests detect only:',
 'L. pneumophila serogroup 1', ['All Legionella species', 'L. micdadei and L. bozemanii', 'Any L. pneumophila serogroup'],
 'The antigen test misses other serogroups and species, so culture on BCYE or PCR is still needed. Serogroup 1 causes most cases.'),
('Burkholderia cepacia complex from cystic fibrosis sputum is recovered best on:',
 'B. cepacia selective agar', ['MacConkey agar alone', 'Thayer-Martin agar', 'CIN agar'],
 'Selective media such as BCSA or OFPBL suppress Pseudomonas and other flora in CF sputum. B. cepacia can be overgrown on routine plates.'),
('Which nonfermenter is intrinsically resistant to carbapenems?',
 'Stenotrophomonas maltophilia', ['Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Acinetobacter baumannii', 'Moraxella catarrhalis'],
 'S. maltophilia carries a chromosomal metallo-beta-lactamase, so carbapenems are never reported; trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the usual drug. P. aeruginosa and Acinetobacter acquire carbapenem resistance.'),
('The preferred specimen for Bordetella pertussis PCR is a:',
 'Nasopharyngeal swab', ['Throat swab', 'Expectorated sputum', 'Saliva sample'],
 'B. pertussis attaches to ciliated cells of the nasopharynx, so a flocked or synthetic NP swab is collected. Throat swabs miss it.'),
('Toxin production by a Corynebacterium diphtheriae isolate is confirmed with:',
 'Elek test', ['Reverse CAMP test', 'Tinsdale agar halo', 'Catalase test'],
 'In the Elek test, toxin from the isolate meets antitoxin in agar and forms precipitin lines. The halo on Tinsdale agar identifies the species but not toxin.'),
('On sorbitol MacConkey agar, Escherichia coli O157:H7 colonies appear:',
 'Colorless', ['Pink', 'Black-centered', 'Blue-green'],
 'O157:H7 does not ferment sorbitol in 24 hours, unlike most E. coli, which form pink colonies. Shiga toxin testing finds non-O157 strains.'),
('Vibrio vulnificus bloodstream infection most often follows:',
 'Eating raw oysters', ['Eating undercooked pork', 'Drinking pond water', 'Handling reptiles'],
 'V. vulnificus lives in warm seawater and shellfish; people with liver disease are at highest risk of sepsis. Yersinia follows pork and Salmonella reptile contact.'),
('Cold enrichment at 4 C helps recover which stool pathogen?',
 'Yersinia enterocolitica', ['Campylobacter jejuni', 'Shigella sonnei', 'Salmonella Typhi'],
 'Yersinia grows at refrigerator temperatures, which favors it over other stool flora. Campylobacter needs 42 C instead.'),
("A Salmonella isolate's serotype is assigned by testing its:",
 'O and H antigens', ['Vi and K antigens', 'Phage type only', 'Biochemical profile'],
 'The Kauffmann-White-Le Minor scheme names serotypes from somatic O and flagellar H antigens. Vi is a capsular antigen used for Salmonella Typhi.'),
('A cat bite wound grows an oxidase-positive gram-negative coccobacillus that does not grow on MacConkey agar. Most likely:',
 'Pasteurella multocida', ['Capnocytophaga canimorsus', 'Eikenella corrodens', 'Bartonella henselae'],
 'P. multocida is part of normal cat and dog mouth flora and causes rapid wound infections. Eikenella follows human bites, and Bartonella is rarely cultured from wounds.'),
('Prenatal group B streptococcus screening uses vaginal-rectal swabs placed into:',
 'Enrichment broth', ['Thioglycolate broth', 'Chocolate agar only', 'Bile esculin broth'],
 'Enrichment broth with antibiotics lets small numbers of S. agalactiae outgrow other flora before subculture or NAAT. Direct plating misses light colonization.'),
('Any growth of a urinary pathogen is significant in which specimen?',
 'Suprapubic aspirate', ['Midstream clean-catch urine', 'Urine from a drainage bag', 'First-void urine'],
 'A suprapubic aspirate bypasses the urethra, so it should be sterile. Voided urine picks up urethral flora; drainage bag urine is never cultured.'),
('Small beta-hemolytic streptococcal colonies with a butterscotch odor from an abscess are:',
 'Streptococcus anginosus group', ['Streptococcus pyogenes', 'Streptococcus agalactiae', 'Enterococcus faecium'],
 'The anginosus group forms tiny colonies with a caramel odor and causes abscesses. S. pyogenes colonies are larger and odorless.'),
('The tube coagulase test detects:',
 'Free coagulase', ['Clumping factor', 'Protein A', 'Thermostable DNase'],
 'Free (extracellular) coagulase acts with coagulase-reacting factor to clot rabbit plasma in the tube. The slide test detects bound clumping factor.'),
('Which pair is not reliably separated by MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry?',
 'Escherichia coli and Shigella', ['Staphylococcus aureus and S. epidermidis', 'Enterococcus faecalis and E. faecium', 'Candida albicans and C. glabrata'],
 'E. coli and Shigella are so closely related that their protein spectra overlap, so other tests separate them. The other pairs have distinct spectra.'),
('Which method identifies a bacterium that defeats both biochemical and MALDI-TOF identification?',
 '16S rRNA sequencing', ['Repeat Gram stain', 'Latex agglutination', 'Serotyping'],
 'The 16S gene has conserved and variable regions, so its sequence matched to a database names most bacteria. Serotyping works only within a known species.'),
('Which test detects carbapenemase production in Enterobacterales?',
 'Modified carbapenem inactivation', ['D-zone test', 'Cefoxitin disk screen', 'Clavulanate combination disk test'],
 'In the mCIM, a meropenem disk soaked in the isolate is inactivated if a carbapenemase is present, so a susceptible indicator strain grows up to it. The D-zone test is for inducible clindamycin resistance.'),
('Aminoglycosides kill bacteria by binding the:',
 '30S ribosomal subunit', ['50S ribosomal subunit', 'Cell wall precursors', 'DNA gyrase'],
 'Binding the 30S subunit causes misreading of mRNA. Macrolides bind the 50S subunit, vancomycin binds cell wall precursors and fluoroquinolones target DNA gyrase.'),
('A tiny gram-negative coccobacillus from a rabbit hunter grows on chocolate but not blood agar after 48 hours. Suspect:',
 'Francisella tularensis', ['Brucella melitensis', 'Yersinia pestis', 'Haemophilus influenzae'],
 'Francisella needs cysteine and grows slowly, and handling it is a major lab infection risk. Sentinel labs rule it out and refer it to the Laboratory Response Network.'),
('A mycobacterium whose colonies turn yellow only after light exposure is a:',
 'Photochromogen', ['Scotochromogen', 'Nonchromogen', 'Rapid grower'],
 'Photochromogens such as M. kansasii make pigment only after light. Scotochromogens such as M. gordonae are pigmented in the dark too.'),
('Which mycobacterium is niacin positive and reduces nitrate?',
 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis', ['Mycobacterium bovis', 'Mycobacterium avium complex', 'Mycobacterium kansasii'],
 'M. tuberculosis accumulates niacin and reduces nitrate. M. bovis is negative for both, and M. kansasii reduces nitrate but is niacin negative.'),
('The preferred laboratory test for herpes simplex encephalitis is:',
 'CSF PCR', ['CSF viral culture', 'Serum HSV IgM', 'Tzanck smear'],
 'PCR finds HSV DNA in CSF with high sensitivity and specificity. HSV rarely grows from CSF, and serology does not show brain infection.'),
('Oocysts of 8 to 10 µm that autofluoresce blue under UV light and stain variably acid-fast are:',
 'Cyclospora cayetanensis', ['Cryptosporidium parvum', 'Cystoisospora belli', 'Giardia duodenalis'],
 'Cyclospora oocysts are twice the size of Cryptosporidium (4 to 6 µm) and autofluoresce. Cystoisospora oocysts are much larger and oval.'),
('Eggs with a terminal spine found in urine are from:',
 'Schistosoma haematobium', ['Schistosoma mansoni', 'Schistosoma japonicum', 'Trichuris trichiura'],
 'S. haematobium lives in bladder veins and sheds terminal-spined eggs into urine, best collected around midday. S. mansoni eggs have a lateral spine and are found in stool.'),
('Thick-walled, spindle-shaped macroconidia with knobbed ends point to:',
 'Microsporum canis', ['Trichophyton rubrum', 'Epidermophyton floccosum', 'Trichophyton tonsurans'],
 'M. canis has large rough spindle macroconidia. Epidermophyton has smooth club-shaped macroconidia in clusters, and Trichophyton species mostly show microconidia.'),
('A yeast that grows at 42 C, is often multidrug resistant and spreads between hospital patients is:',
 'Candida auris', ['Candida albicans', 'Candida parapsilosis', 'Cryptococcus neoformans'],
 'C. auris persists on surfaces and skin, causes outbreaks and can be misidentified by older systems. Isolates are reported to infection prevention and public health.'),
('A mold at 25 C with tuberculate macroconidia converts to small budding yeast at 37 C. It is:',
 'Histoplasma capsulatum', ['Blastomyces dermatitidis', 'Coccidioides immitis', 'Sporothrix schenckii'],
 'Thick-walled knobby macroconidia and tiny intracellular yeast mark Histoplasma. Blastomyces yeast are large with a broad-based bud, and Coccidioides forms spherules.'),
]
